                <div>
                    <p>At that time, <persName xml:id="en-a1" corresp="zh-a1" key="rh0007" reg="Manjusri" type="Bodhisattva">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">Mañjuśrī</foreign>
                        </persName> addressed the <persName key="bg0004" reg="Sakyamuni" type="Buddha" xml:id="en-a2" corresp="zh-a2">Buddha</persName>, and said: “<persName xml:id="en-a3" corresp="zh-a3" key="bg0004" reg="Sakyamuni" type="Buddha">
                            <addName type="honorific" corresp="zh-a4" xml:id="en-a4">World-Honored
                            One</addName>
                        </persName>, I regard the Correct Law as unconditioned, as
                        without attributes, as unobtainable, as without benefit, as unproduced, as
                        unextinguished, as without coming or going, as without knower, as without
                        viewer, as without doer. I do not see <hi rend="italic">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">prajñāpāramitā</foreign>
                        </hi> [Perfection of Wisdom]
                        nor do I see the perceptual sphere of <hi rend="italic">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">prajñāpāramitā</foreign>
                        </hi>. It is neither
                        confirmation nor disconfirmation, it does not engage in frivolous discourse,
                        it is without differentiation. The Law in its entirety is inexhaustible, it
                        transcends exhaustibility. It is without the law of ordinary people, without
                        the law of Voice-Hearers,<ptr target="note_en_1"/> without the law of
                            Pratyeka-Buddhas<ptr target="note_en_2"/> or the law of Buddhas. It is
                        neither obtainable nor unobtainable. It does not relinquish life and death
                        and does not confirm nirvana. It is neither conceivable nor inconceivable,
                        neither conditioned nor unconditioned. If the attributes of the Law are like
                        this, I do not know how one should study <hi rend="italic">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">prajñāpāramitā</foreign>
                        </hi>.” At that time the
                            <persName key="bg0004" reg="Sakyamuni" type="Buddha" xml:id="en-a5" corresp="zh-a5">Buddha</persName> told <persName key="rh0007" reg="Manjusri" type="Bodhisattva" corresp="zh-a6" xml:id="en-a6">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">Mañjuśrī</foreign>
                        </persName>, “If one can
                        in this way know the attributes of the Law, this is named studying <hi rend="italic">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">prajñāpāramitā</foreign>
                        </hi>. If
                        the <foreign xml:lang="sa">bodhisattva-mahāsattva</foreign> wishes to study
                        the Samadhi [absorption] of Bodhi [enlightenment]-Sovereignty, then, after
                        attaining this samadhi, he illuminates all exceedingly deep Buddha laws, and
                        knows without exception all Buddha names, and furthermore thoroughly fathoms
                        all Buddha worlds.”<ptr target="note_en_3"/>
                        <persName key="rh0007" reg="Manjusri" type="Bodhisattva" xml:id="en-a7" corresp="zh-a7">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa"> Mañjuśrī</foreign>
                        </persName> addressed the
                            <persName corresp="zh-a8" xml:id="en-a8" key="bg0004" reg="Sakyamuni" type="Buddha">Buddha</persName> and said: “<persName key="bg0004" reg="Sakyamuni" type="Buddha" corresp="zh-a9" xml:id="en-a9">
                            <addName type="honorific" xml:id="en-a10" corresp="zh-a10">World-Honored
                                One</addName>
                        </persName>, why is it named <hi rend="italic">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">prajñāpāramitā</foreign>
                        </hi>?” The <persName key="bg0004" reg="Sakyamuni" type="Buddha" corresp="zh-a11" xml:id="en-a11">Buddha</persName> said: “<hi rend="italic">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">Prajñāpāramitā</foreign>
                        </hi> is without boundary,
                        without bourn, without name, without attributes, inconceivable, without
                        refuge, without sandbank,<ptr target="note_en_4"/> without sin, without
                        merit, without obscurity, without illumination. Like the Realm of the Law it
                        is without demarcation and also limitless in number. This is named <hi rend="italic">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">prajñāpāramitā</foreign>
                        </hi>. It
                        is also named the Site of Bo­dhi­sattva-<foreign xml:lang="sa">Mahāsattva</foreign> Practice. Since it is neither a site of practice
                        nor of non-practice—all [sites of Bo­dhi­sattva-<foreign xml:lang="sa">Mahāsattva</foreign> Practice] belonging to the One Vehicle—it is named
                        the Site of Non-Practice. Why is this? Because it is unthought and
                        unconditioned.” </p>
                </div></div>
